he interior, captured footage of massive clearings, perfectly circular, hundreds of feet across. Nothing grows there. The dirt is different, darker. Analysis shows the soil is sterile. Nothing can grow there. The clearings are too perfect to be natural. The wildlife is bizarre. In 2012, researchers found a species of chimp that walks upright exclusively. They don't knuckle walk like other chimps. They stand and walk like humans. The footage is unsettling. They use tools in ways chimps shouldn't be able to, creating spears, building structures. There are reports of large unknown primates. Gorillaized, but different, moving through the canopy instead of on the ground. Locals call them different names in different regions, but the descriptions match. Greenland ice sheet. Greenland ice sheet covers 660,000 square miles. The ice is up to 2 m thick. Beneath it is rock, valleys, mountains, lakes, an entire landscape frozen in time. Ground penetrating radar shows structures under the ice. Geometric patterns too regular to be natural formations. In 2018, researchers found a massive crater under the ice, 19 mi wide, caused by a meteorite impact. But the crater is pristine, perfectly preserved. It hit recently, maybe 13,000 years ago. That's nothing in geological time. The impact would have been catastrophic, melting massive amounts of ice, flooding coastlines worldwide. Some scientists think this impact caused ancient flood myths. The ice cores reveal something stranger. Trapped air bubbles from millions of years ago. The atmosphere was completely different. But some bubbles contain modern air. Air from our current atmosphere somehow mixed into ancient ice. The cores also contain microbes frozen for millions of years. When thawed, they come back to life. Ancient bacteria that shouldn't be compatible with modern ecosystems. Beneath the ice sheet radar detects lakes. Liquid water existing under miles of ice. The pressure and geothermal heat keep them liquid. These lakes are connected by rivers flowing beneath the ice. The water is ancient, never exposed to modern atmosphere. Scientists theorized life could exist there, adapted to total darkness and extreme pressure. In 2019, sensors detected movement in these subglacial lakes, large objects moving through the water. The patterns suggest biological activity. Amazon interior. Amazon interior is the deepest part of the Amazon rainforest, areas that have never been entered by outsiders. These zones are protected, inhabited by uncontacted tribes who defend their territory violently. The jungle there is different from the explored regions. Darker, denser, filled with species unknown to science. Camera traps placed at the edge of these zones capture incredible footage. Jaguars that are pure black, no spots, completely melanistic. They're twice the size of normal jaguars. Moving through the undergrowth like ghosts, giant otter 8 ft long, far bigger than known specimens. They hunt in packs, taking down prey much larger than themselves. The trees in the deep interior are massive, some over 300 ft tall, taller than any documented rainforest trees. Their canopies create a second forest layer. Animals live up there that never touch the ground, species of monkeys never photographed, sloths with unusual coloring, birds that don't match any known family. In 2020, acoustic sensors detected sounds from the deep zones. Calls that don't match any cataloged animal. Deep whooping sounds almost like speech. The patterns are complex, too complex to be random. There are stories from tribes at the edge. They speak of things in the deep forest. Large bipeedal creatures covered in hair standing over 7 ft tall. The tribes leave offerings for them. Stay out of certain areas. Every culture has similar stories, different names, but the same descriptions. Mapping the forest giant. Scientists dismiss the stories, but the tribes are adamant. They've seen them, interacted with them. Gambert Mountains. Gambertsef mountains are buried under Antarctic ice, a mountain range no one has ever seen. The peaks rise 9,000 ft, but they're hidden under 2,000 ft of solid ice. Discovered in 1958 by Russian aircraft using instruments. Since then, we've only studied them with radar. The mountains are impossibly old. They should have eroded millions of years ago, but they're still sharp, jagged peaks like they formed yesterday. Scientists don't understand how they've been preserved. The ice should have worn them down. Something about these mountains defies normal geological processes. Radar scans show structures at the base of the mountains. Rectangular shapes too precise to be natural. Some are hundreds of feet long, perfectly aligned, could be rock formations, or could be something else. The scans also show caverns, massive hollow spaces inside the mountains, some big enough to fit stadiums. The caverns appear to connect, forming a network beneath the ice. In 2013, researchers detected radio signals coming from the mountains. Weak signals, but regular, pulsing at consistent intervals. Natural processes don't create regular radio pulses. The signals could be from magnetic anomalies or from something transmitting. The mystery deepens because the signals only occur at specific times. Always when the sun is at certain positions, like the mountains are responding to solar radiation. The ice above the mountains is different. It has fewer impurities, like it formed faster than surrounding ice, or from different water. Core samples show the ice directly above the peaks contains microscopic metal particles titanium chromium metals that shouldn't be concentrated in ice. The distribution suggests the metals came from below, from the mountains themselves. Some researchers speculate the mountains contain mineral deposits unlike anything we've seen. Others think the mountains might not be natural at\",\"language\":\"en\",\"_credits\":{\"used\":5}}\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
